WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
David: Welcome to LitShare.

2
00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:04.850
This is Protein Degradation English
Corner.

3
00:00:04.913 --> 00:00:06.162
Episode two.

4
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:08.825
Nature, Christina M. Woo.

5
00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:12.525
The Degron Thalidomide Was Copying.

6
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:14.575
Here is the question.

7
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:20.625
Thalidomide binds cereblon and changes
which proteins get destroyed.

8
00:00:21.575 --> 00:00:24.025
But cereblon existed long before
thalidomide.

9
00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:27.950
So what does it recognize on its own?

10
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.800
What natural tag was thalidomide copying?

11
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.575
Alex: That is the question Christina Woo's
team at Harvard set out to answer.

12
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.325
Their paper is in Nature, 2022.

13
00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:44.975
First authors Saki Ichikawa, Hope Flaxman,
and Wenqing Xu.

14
00:00:47.325 --> 00:00:55.175
And the answer is a small chemical tag
that forms on proteins as they age: a
C-terminal cyclic imide.

15
00:00:56.075 --> 00:00:59.050
David: Before we get there, what is a
degron?

16
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.650
Alex: A degron is a short signal on a
protein that marks it for destruction.

17
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:12.950
An E3 ligase reads the signal, attaches
ubiquitin, and the proteasome destroys the
protein.

18
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.425
Cereblon is the part of the ligase that
reads the signal.

19
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.175
Thalidomide hijacks it.

20
00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:23.475
David: And nobody knew what cereblon reads
naturally?

21
00:01:24.225 --> 00:01:24.600
Alex: Right.

22
00:01:24.975 --> 00:01:25.875
People had guesses.

23
00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:30.075
Thalidomide binds cereblon through its
glutarimide ring.

24
00:01:30.525 --> 00:01:33.100
So the natural signal might look like that
ring.

25
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:43.500
The team guessed it could be a
modification that forms when a glutamine
or asparagine at the end of a protein
curls up and closes into a ring: a cyclic
imide.

26
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.400
David: How do you test a guess like that?

27
00:01:46.975 --> 00:01:47.850
Alex: With a clever trick.

28
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:48.825
They built degraders.

29
00:01:50.250 --> 00:02:00.025
Take JQ1, a molecule that binds the
protein BRD4, and attach a dipeptide
ending in a cyclic imide instead of
thalidomide.

30
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:06.275
If the cyclic imide engages cereblon, BRD4
gets degraded.

31
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.300
The degradation itself is the readout.

32
00:02:10.175 --> 00:02:11.500
David: And did it work?

33
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:17.550
Alex: First they tried other candidates:
uridine-based molecules, pyroglutamate.

34
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:18.900
Nothing worked.

35
00:02:19.425 --> 00:02:21.750
Then they tested fifteen more candidates.

36
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:28.750
The winners were dipeptides ending in a
glutarimide, the ring from cyclized
glutamine.

37
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:35.500
BRD4 was degraded in a dose-dependent way,
from one nanomolar up.

38
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.425
David: What about the details?

39
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.675
Which amino acid sits next to the ring?

40
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.700
Alex: They tested all twenty amino acids
at that position.

41
00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.950
Almost all of them worked, except
aspartate and glutamate.

42
00:02:50.525 --> 00:02:52.625
Non-polar and aromatic side chains worked
best.

43
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.050
They picked phenylalanine-glutamine, FcQ,
for the rest of the study.

44
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.200
David: How do we know it really goes
through cereblon?

45
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:02.550
Alex: Three checks.

46
00:03:03.175 --> 00:03:05.650
Knocking down cereblon blocked the
degradation.

47
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:11.650
Changing the stereochemistry at any of the
three chiral centers killed the degrader.

48
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.475
And lenalidomide competed with it.

49
00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.600
All three point to the same binding site.

50
00:03:18.075 --> 00:03:20.525
David: What about the asparagine version,
the aspartimide?

51
00:03:21.825 --> 00:03:22.875
Alex: That worked too.

52
00:03:23.525 --> 00:03:29.925
JQ1-FcN degraded BRD4, also through
cereblon, within two hours.

53
00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:35.100
So both the five-membered and six-membered
rings count as degrons.

54
00:03:35.575 --> 00:03:39.550
David: Did they show the molecules
actually bind cereblon directly?

55
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:40.675
Alex: Yes.

56
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:48.775
In a photoaffinity assay, the dipeptides
displaced a light-activated lenalidomide
probe from cereblon.

57
00:03:49.275 --> 00:03:58.550
And in AlphaScreen and NanoBRET assays,
the degraders brought BRD4 and cereblon
together into a ternary complex.

58
00:03:59.575 --> 00:04:00.625
David: Here is the interesting part.

59
00:04:01.875 --> 00:04:04.725
Thalidomide makes cereblon grab new
proteins, like IKZF1.

60
00:04:07.325 --> 00:04:09.475
Do these dipeptides do the same?

61
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.300
Alex: No, and that is a key difference.

62
00:04:13.175 --> 00:04:16.425
The dipeptides did not recruit any new
substrates.

63
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:24.575
In myeloma cells, pomalidomide degraded
IKZF1, but the dipeptides degraded
nothing.

64
00:04:25.375 --> 00:04:32.400
And the myeloma cells were sensitive to
lenalidomide, while the dipeptides had no
effect even at high doses.

65
00:04:33.075 --> 00:04:37.700
David: So the natural tag engages cereblon
without the drug's side effects.

66
00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:38.775
Alex: Exactly.

67
00:04:39.575 --> 00:04:44.750
It binds the same pocket but does not
reshape cereblon the way thalidomide does.

68
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.600
David: But a degrader is a synthetic
molecule.

69
00:04:49.475 --> 00:04:51.925
Does the tag work on a real protein?

70
00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:54.425
Alex: That is the next experiment.

71
00:04:54.850 --> 00:05:00.725
They used sortase, an enzyme, to attach
the cyclic imide to the end of GFP.

72
00:05:01.475 --> 00:05:03.175
Then they watched what happened.

73
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:09.775
In a test tube, the CRBN ligase complex
ubiquitinated the tagged GFP.

74
00:05:10.425 --> 00:05:14.625
The untagged version, with a normal
glutamine, was untouched.

75
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:16.650
The ring is required.

76
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.725
David: And inside cells?

77
00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:23.250
Alex: They delivered the tagged GFP into
human cells.

78
00:05:24.625 --> 00:05:28.325
After six hours, the tagged GFP had
dropped sharply.

79
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.000
Knocking out cereblon, or adding
lenalidomide, rescued it.

80
00:05:34.425 --> 00:05:43.250
And the tag was transferable: it worked on
other proteins too, and it could replace
thalidomide in other degraders.

81
00:05:43.525 --> 00:05:44.900
David: So the tag is real.

82
00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.050
But does it actually occur in the human
body?

83
00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.375
Alex: Yes, and this is where the story
gets big.

84
00:05:52.125 --> 00:05:58.025
Cyclic imides form spontaneously when
asparagine or glutamine residues age.

85
00:05:58.625 --> 00:06:06.050
The team searched proteomics data from
human tissues and found more than a
thousand modification sites, on several
hundred proteins.

86
00:06:08.175 --> 00:06:14.000
The half-life is seventeen to twenty-five
hours, long enough for cereblon to find
them.

87
00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:15.925
David: Where did they find the most?

88
00:06:15.925 --> 00:06:19.125
Alex: In haemoglobin, the protein in red
blood cells.

89
00:06:19.475 --> 00:06:25.200
Red blood cells live about a hundred and
twenty days, and they do not express
cereblon.

90
00:06:25.725 --> 00:06:27.525
So the tags accumulate there.

91
00:06:28.125 --> 00:06:37.375
The team confirmed the modifications in
blood samples from healthy donors, and in
aged bovine eye lenses, where twenty-two
sites turned up.

92
00:06:37.725 --> 00:06:41.025
David: That suggests cereblon is a cleanup
system.

93
00:06:41.675 --> 00:06:42.625
Alex: That is the model.

94
00:06:43.075 --> 00:06:44.375
They tested it directly.

95
00:06:45.075 --> 00:06:51.200
They made a haemoglobin chain carrying the
tag and showed the CRBN complex
ubiquitinates it.

96
00:06:51.800 --> 00:07:00.150
Then they blocked cereblon in cells,
either by knocking it out or with
lenalidomide, and the cyclic imide
fragments piled up.

97
00:07:02.875 --> 00:07:05.775
In one experiment, thirty-four out of
thirty-six tagged peptides increased.

98
00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:07.625
David: How big was the increase?

99
00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:20.975
Alex: For one well-measured site on actin,
the tag rose by about a quarter to a third
in kidney cells, and by about three-
quarters in myeloma cells, after two days
of lenalidomide.

100
00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:24.175
David: What does this mean for the field?

101
00:07:24.175 --> 00:07:26.375
Alex: It reframes cereblon.

102
00:07:27.075 --> 00:07:28.925
It may not just be a drug target.

103
00:07:29.475 --> 00:07:35.050
It may be a quality-control system that
removes damaged proteins before they pile
up.

104
00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:43.425
That fits with the observation that loss
of cereblon's thalidomide-binding domain
is linked to intellectual disability.

105
00:07:44.175 --> 00:07:46.250
The damage matters most in the brain.

106
00:07:46.312 --> 00:07:48.062
David: What is still missing?

107
00:07:48.725 --> 00:07:49.375
Alex: Three things.

108
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.850
First, everything here is in cells and in
test tubes.

109
00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.225
There is no animal data yet.

110
00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:02.900
Second, we do not know how the hydrolysed
fragments are cleared if cereblon misses
them.

111
00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:10.975
And third, we do not know whether the tags
form purely by chance, or whether enzymes
help make them.

112
00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:22.600
David: One sentence to remember:
thalidomide was copying a natural cleanup
tag, a cyclic imide that marks ageing
proteins for destruction by cereblon.

113
00:08:23.375 --> 00:08:28.475
You have been listening to Protein
Degradation English Corner, from Litshare.

114
00:08:29.225 --> 00:08:30.250
See you next time.
